1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,617
August 26, 2008. Nist headquarters, Gaithersburg, Maryland.

2
00:00:03,617 --> 00:00:12,062
Nearly seven years after the attack, the government's lead investigator stands in front of a live-streamed technical briefing.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,379
His name is Dr. Shyam Sunder. He starts a computer simulation.

4
00:00:17,379 --> 00:00:21,559
On the screen: one steel column, identified only by a number.

5
00:00:22,699 --> 00:00:27,061
Column seventy nine. Two hundred feet in the air, inside Building 7.

6
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:36,872
He is about to explain how that piece of ordinary structural steel, heated by nothing more than an office fire, quietly walked off its own bearing seat.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,359
And took a forty seven story building down with it — the one building, of the three this investigation covers, that no aircraft ever struck.

8
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,772
Two thousand, nine hundred seventy seven people were killed that morning.

9
00:00:50,772 --> 00:00:53,450
This is not the story of how that happened.

10
00:00:53,450 --> 00:00:59,699
This is the story of why three buildings actually came down — told from the investigation's own findings, not the internet's.

11
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:02,936
One honest rule before we start.

12
00:01:02,936 --> 00:01:09,247
Every finding in this episode traces back to Nist's own federal investigation, or to a named, on-the-record source.

13
00:01:09,247 --> 00:01:14,155
Where something is genuinely disputed, we will say so, on screen, every single time.

14
00:01:15,299 --> 00:01:17,102
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

15
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:22,517
The World Trade Center collapse. What the investigation actually found.

16
00:01:23,666 --> 00:01:30,014
To understand why these buildings fell, you first have to understand how, and why, they were built this way.

17
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:38,170
The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey broke ground on the World Trade Center complex in 1966.

18
00:01:38,170 --> 00:01:42,984
The first tenants moved into the still-unfinished North Tower in December of 1970.

19
00:01:42,984 --> 00:01:47,799
The formal dedication came on April 4, 1973, with Governor Nelson Rockefeller presiding.

20
00:01:48,933 --> 00:01:51,198
Twin towers, one hundred ten stories each.

21
00:01:51,198 --> 00:01:58,965
The North Tower rose one thousand three hundred sixty eight feet to its roof; the South Tower, one thousand three hundred sixty two feet.

22
00:01:58,965 --> 00:02:06,409
For a brief stretch, they stood among the tallest buildings in the world — until Chicago's Sears Tower passed them, that same year.

23
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:12,940
For nearly three decades after that, these two towers were simply what New York's skyline looked like.

24
00:02:14,066 --> 00:02:18,458
The architect was Minoru Yamasaki, working with Emery Roth and Sons.

25
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:32,430
The structural engineering firm was Worthington, Skilling, Helle and Jackson — John Skilling as chief structural engineer, Leslie Robertson as lead project structural engineer, the man most closely credited historically with the towers' structural system.

26
00:02:33,566 --> 00:02:38,238
And that system is the single most important engineering fact in this whole episode.

27
00:02:38,238 --> 00:02:49,919
Per Nist's own final report: a perimeter tube of two hundred thirty six closely spaced steel columns, fifty nine per face, tied together at every floor, carrying nearly all of the towers' lateral wind load.

28
00:02:51,066 --> 00:02:59,081
A separate central core, forty seven to fifty one columns, carried gravity loads only — elevators, stairwells, utility risers.

29
00:02:59,081 --> 00:03:02,455
That core was never designed to resist wind.

30
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:13,108
Bridging the sixty foot span between the tube and the core: lightweight, prefabricated steel bar-joist floor trusses, under a thin, four inch concrete floor slab.

31
00:03:13,108 --> 00:03:19,573
The Port Authority wanted column-free office floors, and got them — forty thousand square feet, per floor.

32
00:03:19,573 --> 00:03:26,039
The tradeoff: markedly less structural steel per square foot than a conventional, heavier skyscraper of that era.

33
00:03:27,166 --> 00:03:32,632
One more building matters to this story, and it is not a third twin tower.

34
00:03:32,632 --> 00:03:41,377
Seven World Trade Center was a separate building entirely — completed fourteen years later, in 1987, on a different footprint, north of the plaza.

35
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:52,684
It used a genuinely different structural system: an exterior moment-resisting frame, four perimeter moment frames and two-story belt trusses — not the towers' tube-and-core design.

36
00:03:52,684 --> 00:03:57,573
Remember that distinction. The two structures fail for two completely different reasons.

37
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:01,477
This building had weathered an attack before.

38
00:04:01,477 --> 00:04:09,413
On February 26, 1993, a truck bomb detonated on an underground parking level beneath the North Tower, killing six people.

39
00:04:09,413 --> 00:04:13,381
The towers survived structurally, and reopened for eight more years.

40
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:17,399
What happened that morning is documented in detail elsewhere.

41
00:04:17,399 --> 00:04:21,266
Here, we cover only what the impact did to the buildings' steel.

42
00:04:22,399 --> 00:04:31,153
American Airlines Flight 11 struck the North Tower at eight forty six and forty seconds in the morning, between floors ninety three and ninety nine.

43
00:04:31,153 --> 00:04:40,606
United Airlines Flight one seventy five struck the South Tower seventeen minutes later, at nine oh three and eleven seconds, between floors seventy seven and eighty five.

44
00:04:41,733 --> 00:04:51,528
In the North Tower, roughly half the exterior columns on the impact face were severed or damaged: thirty four severed outright, four more heavily damaged.

45
00:04:51,528 --> 00:04:55,055
Three core columns were severed, four more heavily damaged.

46
00:04:56,199 --> 00:05:01,471
In the South Tower: twenty nine exterior columns severed, one more heavily damaged.

47
00:05:01,471 --> 00:05:04,715
Five core columns severed, four more heavily damaged.

48
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:09,610
But the impacts did something else, just as important.

49
00:05:09,610 --> 00:05:18,842
They stripped fireproofing from steel far beyond the columns actually severed — trusses, connections, surviving core columns, left bare, or barely coated.

50
00:05:19,966 --> 00:05:24,417
Here is Nist's own conclusion, stated plainly in its final report.

51
00:05:24,417 --> 00:05:38,173
Quote: the towers 'would likely not have collapsed under the combined effects of aircraft impact damage and the extensive, multi-floor fires alone.' Dislodged fireproofing is a named, necessary third factor — not an afterthought.

52
00:05:39,299 --> 00:05:47,972
That is the combination the next chapter has to explain: impact, lost fireproofing, and fire, acting together on two very different towers.

53
00:05:49,099 --> 00:05:53,070
Inside both towers, fire became the next chapter of this story.

54
00:05:54,199 --> 00:05:57,547
The jet fuel itself burned off within minutes.

55
00:05:57,547 --> 00:06:12,193
What sustained the fires after that was ordinary building contents — furniture, paper, carpet — spreading across roughly forty thousand square feet, on multiple floors, reaching up to a thousand degrees Celsius in localized areas.

56
00:06:12,193 --> 00:06:16,795
Comparable, in that sense, to other unsuppressed high-rise fires in history.

57
00:06:17,933 --> 00:06:21,622
Here is the single most important correction in this whole story.

58
00:06:21,622 --> 00:06:22,964
No structural steel melted.

59
00:06:22,964 --> 00:06:31,350
Steel does not need to melt to fail — bare steel at a thousand degrees Celsius keeps only about ten percent of its room-temperature strength.

60
00:06:31,350 --> 00:06:34,704
The fires did not melt the steel. They weakened it.

61
00:06:35,833 --> 00:06:42,999
With its fireproofing gone, the sagging floor trusses — still tied to the exterior wall — pulled the perimeter columns inward.

62
00:06:42,999 --> 00:06:51,872
Nist measured about fifty five inches of inward bow on the North Tower's south face, at floor ninety seven, first visible roughly five minutes before collapse.

63
00:06:52,999 --> 00:06:58,739
Collapse initiated in the South Tower at nine fifty nine that morning — fifty six minutes after impact.

64
00:06:58,739 --> 00:07:03,840
The North Tower followed at ten twenty eight — a hundred two minutes after being hit.

65
00:07:04,966 --> 00:07:14,762
The South Tower came down first, despite a shorter fire, because its impact geometry damaged core columns more directly, and its fire spread less evenly across the floor.

66
00:07:15,899 --> 00:07:17,280
One honest scope note.

67
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:22,114
Nist's structural analysis modeled collapse initiation — not a floor-by-floor simulation of everything after.

68
00:07:22,114 --> 00:07:26,257
For why the fall was total, Nist makes an energy argument instead.

69
00:07:26,257 --> 00:07:31,781
The undamaged structure below could have absorbed the sudden load of only about six additional floors.

70
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:35,924
Twelve to twenty nine floors of upper-building mass came down on it.

71
00:07:35,924 --> 00:07:41,447
In Nist's own words: 'neither tower could have arrested the progression of collapse once collapse initiated.'

72
00:07:42,566 --> 00:07:46,270
The North Tower followed the same physics, roughly half an hour later.

73
00:07:46,270 --> 00:07:54,295
Once its own core and perimeter columns lost the capacity to carry the floors above, nothing in the design below it could stop what came next.

74
00:07:55,433 --> 00:07:59,409
One clarification worth making now, because it comes up again later.

75
00:07:59,409 --> 00:08:06,276
Aircraft aluminum does melt, around five hundred to six hundred degrees Celsius — well below the fire's own temperature.

76
00:08:06,276 --> 00:08:13,504
Mixed with burning carpet and furniture, that is what produced the glowing material later seen falling from the South Tower.

77
00:08:13,504 --> 00:08:17,480
Structural steel itself never reached its own, far higher, melting point.

78
00:08:18,599 --> 00:08:21,329
Two buildings gone, by half past ten that morning.

79
00:08:21,329 --> 00:08:29,519
But the story of what happened to a third building nearby — one nobody thought was in danger at all — took until that evening to finish.

80
00:08:30,666 --> 00:08:33,605
Seven World Trade Center was never struck by an aircraft.

81
00:08:33,605 --> 00:08:36,544
This is the story of how it came down anyway.

82
00:08:37,666 --> 00:08:46,260
When the North Tower collapsed, falling debris struck Building 7's lower floors, damaging its structure and igniting fires on multiple levels.

83
00:08:47,399 --> 00:08:57,004
That same falling debris severed the city water main that served as both the primary, and the backup, source for the building's standpipe and sprinkler systems, on floors one through twenty.

84
00:08:57,004 --> 00:09:00,721
There was no working fire suppression on the floors that mattered most.

85
00:09:01,866 --> 00:09:14,143
Fires burned uncontrolled for roughly seven hours, concentrated on the building's east side — floors seven through nine, and eleven through thirteen — before the building collapsed at five twenty and fifty two seconds that evening.

86
00:09:15,266 --> 00:09:19,612
Nist's own words explain exactly why this case draws so much scrutiny.

87
00:09:19,612 --> 00:09:34,100
Quote: 'the fires on multiple floors in Building 7 were uncontrolled but otherwise similar to fires experienced in other tall buildings, and caused an extraordinary event.' The first known instance of fire alone bringing down a tall steel structure, completely.

88
00:09:35,233 --> 00:09:39,641
The mechanism itself is not simple weakening. It is thermal expansion.

89
00:09:39,641 --> 00:09:55,269
Long-span steel floor beams and girders in Building 7's core — especially around floor thirteen — were heated unevenly, and expanded, at temperatures Nist itself calls 'hundreds of degrees below those typically considered in current practice for fire-resistance ratings.'

90
00:09:56,399 --> 00:10:04,424
One heated floor-thirteen girder, spanning between Column forty four and Column seventy nine, expanded enough to lose its seated connection entirely.

91
00:10:04,424 --> 00:10:05,952
It did not fracture.

92
00:10:05,952 --> 00:10:12,831
It walked off its own bearing seat — leaving Column seventy nine laterally unsupported, over roughly nine stories.

93
00:10:13,966 --> 00:10:15,157
The column buckled.

94
00:10:15,157 --> 00:10:23,098
That triggered a cascading progression of floor failures — upward, then outward across the core, and finally the exterior frame.

95
00:10:23,098 --> 00:10:29,848
The first visible sign, on video, was the building's own east mechanical penthouse, dropping into the structure.

96
00:10:30,966 --> 00:10:35,137
Dr. Shyam Sunder's own summary of the finding, in his own words.

97
00:10:35,137 --> 00:10:43,479
Quote: 'heating of floor beams and girders caused a critical support column to fail, initiating a fire-induced progressive collapse that brought the building down.'

98
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:48,120
Nist states this plainly, to correct a common misconception.

99
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:55,945
Quote: 'neither explosives nor fuel oil fires played a role in the collapse of Building 7.' Not diesel generator fuel.

100
00:10:55,945 --> 00:11:01,031
Ordinary office contents, burning for seven hours, doing something nobody had documented before.

101
00:11:02,166 --> 00:11:11,688
Nist's own investigators found no evidence at all — no witness reports, no video, no structural analysis — suggesting explosives had been used in the first place.

102
00:11:11,688 --> 00:11:16,272
That is why they never even tested the recovered steel for explosive residue.

103
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:20,743
Keep the two mechanisms separate in your head.

104
00:11:20,743 --> 00:11:27,430
Towers One and Two: aircraft impact, lost fireproofing, and fire, acting together on a tube-and-core frame.

105
00:11:27,430 --> 00:11:33,282
Building 7: an ordinary office fire alone, acting on a completely different, moment-frame design.

106
00:11:33,282 --> 00:11:36,625
Two different buildings. Two different chains of cause.

107
00:11:37,766 --> 00:11:41,400
That is the mechanism, according to the government's own investigation.

108
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:50,122
What follows is how that investigation actually reached it — because the story of the finding is almost as contested as the finding itself.

109
00:11:51,266 --> 00:11:55,628
All of this came from one federal investigation, running for six years.

110
00:11:56,766 --> 00:12:04,159
The Federal Building and Fire Safety Investigation of the World Trade Center Disaster was conducted under the National Construction Safety Team Act.

111
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:11,927
The towers' final report, Nist's NCSTAR one, was released at a congressional hearing on October 26, 2005.

112
00:12:11,927 --> 00:12:31,422
Building 7's report followed a longer road: an August 21, 2008 press briefing announcing the finding, an August 26 technical briefing walking through it in full — the one this episode opened on — a draft released for public comment on September 3, 2008, and a final report that November.

113
00:12:32,566 --> 00:12:35,197
The lead investigator, throughout, was Dr.

114
00:12:35,197 --> 00:12:38,705
Shyam Sunder, then chief of Nist's Structures Division.

115
00:12:38,705 --> 00:12:44,843
His team drew on roughly two hundred experts, across Nist staff, industry, and academia.

116
00:12:45,966 --> 00:12:58,897
They reviewed two hundred thirty six recovered structural steel components, more than seven thousand photographs and video segments, and conducted roughly one thousand two hundred interviews — survivors, and emergency responders.

117
00:13:00,033 --> 00:13:04,517
Metallurgist Dr. Frank Gayle led the steel analysis, from 2002 to 2007.

118
00:13:04,517 --> 00:13:10,496
And here, in Nist's own current words, is the finding, stated as plainly as it gets.

119
00:13:10,496 --> 00:13:26,939
Quote: 'in no instance did NIST report that steel in the towers melted due to the fires.' Unprotected steel at those temperatures loses roughly ninety percent of its room-temperature strength — enough to explain the sagging and buckling, without any of it actually melting.

120
00:13:28,066 --> 00:13:42,604
Congress itself held early hearings within months of the attacks, titled, plainly, 'Learning From Nine Eleven: Understanding the Collapse of the World Trade Center.' No official video survives from those 2002 hearings — only the printed transcript does.

121
00:13:42,604 --> 00:13:46,430
It is what set Nist's own multi-year investigation in motion.

122
00:13:47,566 --> 00:13:50,733
That federal investigation did not just publish one finding.

123
00:13:50,733 --> 00:13:59,530
Over time, it also corrected itself, in public, on the record — and that correction is the part of this story people argue about most.

124
00:14:00,666 --> 00:14:04,872
This is the single most misunderstood finding in the entire investigation.

125
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:19,401
Nist's own final report documents Building 7's exterior north face descending in three measured stages: a slower start, then a stretch matching the exact rate of gravity, then a final stretch of decreasing speed, as resistance returned.

126
00:14:20,533 --> 00:14:22,962
Eight stories, in true free fall.

127
00:14:24,100 --> 00:14:34,786
Nist's own explanation: the interior core columns beneath that section — including Column seventy nine and its neighbors — had already buckled and failed, nearly simultaneously, across multiple floors.

128
00:14:34,786 --> 00:14:41,419
So for that brief window, the descending exterior face met almost no resistance from the structure below it.

129
00:14:42,566 --> 00:14:46,675
Be precise about what that measurement does, and does not, mean.

130
00:14:46,675 --> 00:14:51,531
It confirms gravity briefly took over on one section of one building's exterior.

131
00:14:51,531 --> 00:14:59,002
It does not, on its own, mean the entire building's structural system failed all at once, through synchronized demolition charges.

132
00:14:59,002 --> 00:15:01,243
Those are two very different claims.

133
00:15:02,366 --> 00:15:08,174
And here is the sequence, reported as fact, not softened in either direction.

134
00:15:08,174 --> 00:15:12,195
At Nist's own August 21, 2008 press briefing, Dr.

135
00:15:12,195 --> 00:15:18,449
Sunder said free fall would be 'physically impossible,' given the structural resistance he expected.

136
00:15:18,449 --> 00:15:24,257
The final report, that November, then documented and quantified the free-fall phase directly.

137
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:31,017
The person most responsible for that correction was a physics teacher named David Chandler.

138
00:15:31,017 --> 00:15:39,846
During Nist's September 2008 public comment window, Chandler submitted his own solo comment, backed by his own, extensively documented, frame-by-frame video analysis.

139
00:15:40,966 --> 00:15:52,503
A separate, group submission, that same window — led by James Gourley, of the International Center for Nine Eleven Studies — was co-signed by eight people, including physicist Steven Jones.

140
00:15:52,503 --> 00:16:01,733
That group's own frame-by-frame analysis is credited, in the record, to 'members of this group,' collectively — not to any one of them individually.

141
00:16:02,866 --> 00:16:11,421
If Steven Jones's name comes up again in this story, it is for something else entirely — a separate paper, about a different substance altogether.

142
00:16:11,421 --> 00:16:13,132
Do not confuse the two.

143
00:16:14,266 --> 00:16:28,137
Between a real, measured anomaly, a government walk-back, and a topic this contested, it's worth pausing to address, directly, what people actually claim about these buildings — and what the documented record actually says back.

144
00:16:29,266 --> 00:16:34,517
A few claims about this show up constantly online. Let's address them, one at a time.

145
00:16:35,666 --> 00:16:39,217
Building 7 fell at free-fall speed for over two seconds.

146
00:16:39,217 --> 00:16:43,477
Some ask: if explosives did that, why did nobody hear a blast?

147
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:49,655
Nist's own FAQ: that blast would hit one hundred forty decibels, half a mile out.

148
00:16:49,655 --> 00:16:55,048
Nothing like that is on tape. The free fall is real — not proof of demolition.

149
00:16:56,166 --> 00:17:00,032
Others cite molten metal, and a study claiming thermite in the dust.

150
00:17:01,166 --> 00:17:03,282
That glow is aluminum, not steel.

151
00:17:03,282 --> 00:17:06,456
The thermite paper's own editor distanced himself from it.

152
00:17:06,456 --> 00:17:10,335
Nist says it would take a hundred pounds per column, undetected.

153
00:17:11,466 --> 00:17:17,108
Then there's Larry Silverstein's own words, on camera: 'maybe the smartest thing to do is pull it.'

154
00:17:18,233 --> 00:17:21,664
What 'it' means is disputed. His spokesman says the firefighters.

155
00:17:21,664 --> 00:17:25,782
Others read it as demolition language. We are not picking a side.

156
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:32,542
The BBC once reported Building 7 had collapsed — while it still stood on screen behind the reporter.

157
00:17:33,666 --> 00:17:35,247
A real, documented error.

158
00:17:35,247 --> 00:17:40,781
Wire services were already reporting it as down; London passed that along too soon.

159
00:17:40,781 --> 00:17:42,757
A timing mistake, not foreknowledge.

160
00:17:43,900 --> 00:17:49,621
In 2020, a funded university study argued fire alone doesn't explain the collapse.

161
00:17:50,766 --> 00:17:55,848
Funded by a group that already rejects Nist's findings. Never peer reviewed.

162
00:17:55,848 --> 00:17:58,812
Nist's finding remains the official, government-adopted position.

163
00:17:59,933 --> 00:18:04,452
The investigation didn't end with a finding. It ended with a to-do list.

164
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:13,351
Nist issued roughly thirty recommendations — covering structural integrity, fire endurance, active fire protection, emergency response, and evacuation.

165
00:18:13,351 --> 00:18:19,811
The Building 7 report added one more, and reiterated a dozen of the earlier ones.

166
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:24,405
Those recommendations drove more than forty major changes to U.S.

167
00:18:24,405 --> 00:18:32,045
model building and fire codes — twenty three into the International Code Council's own codes, fifteen into the National Fire Protection Association's.

168
00:18:32,045 --> 00:18:37,601
Nist itself calls the pace 'history-making.' One qualifier matters, though: these are changes to model codes.

169
00:18:37,601 --> 00:18:46,977
Individual states and cities still had to adopt each one into their own local law, on their own timeline — not all at once, and not automatically.

170
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:50,150
Some of what actually changed.

171
00:18:50,150 --> 00:18:56,301
Buildings must now resist disproportionate progressive collapse — a direct answer to Column seventy nine.

172
00:18:56,301 --> 00:19:01,222
A full extra hour of required fire resistance, for tall building frames.

173
00:19:01,222 --> 00:19:05,733
Fireproofing bond-strength standards, raised several times over, with new field inspections.

174
00:19:06,866 --> 00:19:14,869
Stairwells, up to half again wider, in new sprinklered high-rises, with a mandatory third stairway in the tallest buildings.

175
00:19:14,869 --> 00:19:20,345
Elevators built for firefighter access — and now permitted for occupant evacuation, too.

176
00:19:21,466 --> 00:19:33,352
One World Trade Center's own design answers this investigation directly: extra-wide stairways, inside a reinforced, positively-pressurized concrete core, built specifically to keep smoke out during a fire.

177
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:48,255
The National September Eleventh Memorial — two reflecting pools, set inside the original towers' own footprints — carries the names of all two thousand, nine hundred seventy seven people killed that day, and the six killed in 1993.

178
00:19:48,255 --> 00:19:54,771
It was dedicated on the tenth anniversary, September 11, 2011, and opened to the public the next day.

179
00:19:55,900 --> 00:20:04,494
The museum beneath it was dedicated in May of 2014, in a ceremony led by then-President Obama, and opened to the public a week later.

180
00:20:05,633 --> 00:20:19,112
One World Trade Center itself — designed by David Childs, following Daniel Libeskind's original master plan — topped out at a symbolic one thousand seven hundred seventy six feet, in May of 2013, and opened that November.

181
00:20:20,233 --> 00:20:29,559
The site today also includes the Oculus, Santiago Calatrava's transit hub, and the Perelman Performing Arts Center — the newest addition, completed in 2023.

182
00:20:30,700 --> 00:20:40,888
Twenty years on, this is what remains: a rebuilt skyline, a set of engineering rules nobody wants tested again, and a case file, still open to anyone willing to actually read it.

183
00:20:42,033 --> 00:20:45,370
What stays with me about this one isn't the internet argument.

184
00:20:45,370 --> 00:20:49,922
It's a bearing seat, on a girder, on a floor most people can't even name.

185
00:20:51,066 --> 00:20:58,032
Nist spent six years, and reviewed two hundred thirty six pieces of recovered steel, to answer a question this specific.

186
00:20:58,032 --> 00:21:01,515
That kind of patience deserves better than a comment section.

